@*
    404Page.razor
    Purpose: The site's "page not found" screen, for BOTH the explicit /404 URL
    and any address that matches no other route.

    REQ-UI-048 — rebuilt on TrBlazeUI.
    Also fixes a dead link: the action pointed at "dashboard-default", which is
    not a route in this application.

    REQ-UI-012 — WHY THIS PAGE HAS ONE PLAIN ROUTE AND NOT A CATCH-ALL.
    This is a Blazor Web App: `MapRazorComponents` registers ONE endpoint per
    `@page` directive it discovers, so a URL that matches no directive matches
    no endpoint at all. ASP.NET routing then ends the request with a bare 404
    and an EMPTY body — the Blazor router never runs, which is why the
    `<NotFound>` fragment in Routes.razor could never fire and a real visitor
    who mistyped a URL got a blank white page (verified 2026-08-09: HTTP 404,
    zero bytes).

    The first fix (2026-08-09) added a catch-all `@page "/{*NotFoundPath}"`
    here. It did render the page, but it traded one defect for two:
      * nothing 404s any more, so an unknown URL answered HTTP 200 — a soft
        404, which search engines index and monitoring cannot see. The page
        cannot correct that itself: the router is mounted
        `@rendermode="InteractiveServer"` (App.razor), so this component never
        receives the HttpContext cascading parameter and cannot set a status.
      * the catch-all matched every unrouted path, including `/uploads/<file>`.
        The static-file middleware stands aside once an endpoint has been
        selected, so an image uploaded at RUNTIME — absent from the build-time
        static-asset manifest, therefore with no endpoint of its own — was
        answered with this page as text/html instead of the image (REQ-FN-025).

    So the catch-all was removed (2026-08-09, cluster F) and the job moved to
    the pipeline, where the status code lives: `UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute`
    in source/TechieBlog/Program.cs re-executes the request against this route
    while PRESERVING the original 404. Unmatched URLs now answer 404 WITH this
    page rendered, and `/uploads/...` reaches the static-file handler again.
    `/404` stays a real route so the page can also be linked to directly — and
    because the re-execution targets exactly this URL: renaming it without
    updating `NotFoundPage.Path` in Program.cs sends the body back to empty.

    Layout: MainLayout — the public shell (header, nav, footer) so a lost
    visitor has a way back into the site. It previously used AuthLayout, which
    renders no navigation at all.
*@

@page "/404"
@layout BlogUI.Layouts.MainLayout

<PageTitle>Page not found - TechieBlog</PageTitle>

<div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-4 py-16 text-center" data-testid="not-found-page">
    <p class="m-0 text-6xl font-bold tracking-tight text-muted-foreground">404</p>
    <h1 class="m-0 text-2xl font-bold">This page wandered off</h1>
    <p class="m-0 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        The page you are looking for might have been removed or renamed.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
        <Button Href="/" data-testid="not-found-home">
            Return to website
        </Button>
        <Button Href="/search" Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" data-testid="not-found-search">
            <LucideIcon Name="search" Size="16" />
            Search the blog
        </Button>
    </div>
</div>

@*
    No route parameter and no echo of the requested path, deliberately: the value is
    visitor-controlled text and reflecting it would be a reflected-content surface for no benefit.
    The path the visitor asked for is already in the request log line via the correlation id.
*@
